; Rosetta - threading cycle for the main panel's Threading tab.
; Called from MDI by Rosetta_main.py's Thread_Run - not meant to be run by hand.
; Created 24 September 2026
;
; Cuts the same passes LinuxCNC's G76 would - first pass at depth J,
; each later pass J deeper (G76's default R1.0 constant-depth degression,
; straight in-feed, Q0) then a pass at the final depth K, then H spring
; passes at K - but as coordinated Z+C helical moves, not spindle-synced
; motion: the work is turned by the C axis, not a spindle, and G76 needs
; spindle.0 index/position feedback this machine doesn't have. Each start
; is cut to full depth, then C indexes 360 / starts degrees to the next.
;
; Everything is incremental from where the cutter is when this is called:
; at the start of the thread, #2 (clearance) off the work surface.
;
; #1  in-feed direction along X: -1 outside threads, +1 inside threads
; #2  clearance, cutter tip to work surface - G76's I
; #3  signed Z travel per pass - thread length, in the cut direction
; #4  signed C travel per pass, degrees - thread length / lead x 360
; #5  C return per pass, degrees - the smallest move putting C back in
;     phase with the start of the pass, so C doesn't rewind whole turns
; #6  first pass depth - G76's J
; #7  final depth - G76's K
; #8  spring passes - G76's H
; #9  number of starts
; #10 C index between starts, degrees - 360 / starts
; #11 X in-feed feed rate, units / min
; #12 inverse-time feed for each helical pass, 1 / minutes
; #13 units - 20 inch, 21 mm
;
; Rosetta_main.py restores the operator's own G20/G21, G90/G91, G93/G94, G7/G8
; and F once this returns or is aborted.
;
; Progress goes out on motion.analog-out-00 (wired to the panel's
; gladevcp.thread-status in Rosetta_PostGUI_v1.hal, shown on the Threading
; tab) as start x 10000 + kind x 1000 + pass number, where kind is
;   1 a pass short of the final depth
;   2 the pass at the final depth
;   3 a spring pass (numbered from 1)
;   8 indexing C to the next start (start = the one it's indexing to)
;   9 finished cutting - returning C to where it began
; M67 rather than M68, so each change lands as that move actually starts,
; not when the interpreter reads ahead to it. 0 = nothing reported yet.

o<rosetta_thread> sub
  o100 if [#13 EQ 21]
    G21
  o100 else
    G20
  o100 endif
  G8 G91 G94
  M68 E0 Q0

  #<start> = 0
  o110 while [#<start> LT #9]
    #<pass> = 1
    #<depth> = #6
    #<done> = 0
    o120 while [#<done> EQ 0]
      o121 if [#<depth> GE #7]
        #<depth> = #7
        #<done> = 1
      o121 endif
      M67 E0 Q[[#<start> + 1] * 10000 + [1 + #<done>] * 1000 + #<pass>]
      G94 G1 X[#1 * [#2 + #<depth>]] F#11
      G93 G1 Z#3 C#4 F#12
      G94 G0 X[-#1 * [#2 + #<depth>]]
      G0 Z[-#3] C#5
      #<pass> = [#<pass> + 1]
      #<depth> = [#6 * #<pass>]
    o120 endwhile

    #<spring> = 0
    o130 repeat [#8]
      #<spring> = [#<spring> + 1]
      M67 E0 Q[[#<start> + 1] * 10000 + 3000 + #<spring>]
      G94 G1 X[#1 * [#2 + #7]] F#11
      G93 G1 Z#3 C#4 F#12
      G94 G0 X[-#1 * [#2 + #7]]
      G0 Z[-#3] C#5
    o130 endrepeat

    ; Index to the next start - after the last start this brings C back
    ; in phase with where it began.
    o140 if [#<start> LT [#9 - 1]]
      M67 E0 Q[[#<start> + 2] * 10000 + 8000]
    o140 else
      M67 E0 Q[[#<start> + 1] * 10000 + 9000]
    o140 endif
    G0 C#10
    #<start> = [#<start> + 1]
  o110 endwhile
o<rosetta_thread> endsub
M2
